<h1>Conventional Routing in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    Conventional Routing is the default routing mechanism in ASP.NET Core MVC
    where routes are defined centrally inside <strong>Program.cs</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    It follows a predefined URL pattern to map incoming requests
    to controllers and action methods.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Where Conventional Routing is Defined?</h2>

<p>
    Conventional routing is configured inside:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Program.cs
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Understanding the Route Pattern</h2>

<pre>
<code>
{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This route pattern contains three parts:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Controller</li>
    <li>Action</li>
    <li>Route Parameter</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>1. Controller Segment</h2>

<pre>
<code>
{controller=Home}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    The first URL segment maps to the controller.
</p>

<p>
    If no controller is provided,
    MVC uses the default controller:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
HomeController
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    MVC searches for:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
ProductController
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>2. Action Segment</h2>

<pre>
<code>
{action=Index}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    The second URL segment maps to the action method.
</p>

<p>
    If no action is provided,
    MVC uses the default action:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Index()
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    MVC searches for:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
public IActionResult Details()
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>3. Route Parameter</h2>

<pre>
<code>
{id?}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This represents an optional route parameter.
</p>

<p>
    The <strong>?</strong> symbol means the parameter is optional.
</p>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    MVC passes:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
id = 5
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Full Practical Example</h2>

<h3>Step 1 — Create Controller</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public class ProductController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return Content("Product Index");
    }

    public IActionResult Details(int id)
    {
        return Content($"Product Id: {id}");
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 2 — Configure Route</h3>

<pre>
<code>
app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 3 — Test URLs</h3>

<p>
    URL:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Output:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Product Index
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<p>
    URL:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Output:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Product Id: 5
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How Conventional Routing Works Internally</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Browser Request
        ↓
Routing Middleware
        ↓
Controller Selection
        ↓
Action Selection
        ↓
Parameter Binding
        ↓
Action Executes
        ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Advantages of Conventional Routing</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Simple to configure</li>
    <li>Centralized routing setup</li>
    <li>Easy for MVC applications</li>
    <li>Less repetitive code</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Disadvantages of Conventional Routing</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Less flexible for custom URLs</li>
    <li>Can become difficult in large APIs</li>
    <li>Not ideal for highly customized routing</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Conventional Routing vs Attribute Routing</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Conventional Routing</th>
        <th>Attribute Routing</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Defined in Program.cs</td>
        <td>Defined on Controllers/Actions</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Centralized</td>
        <td>Distributed</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Best for MVC Apps</td>
        <td>Best for APIs</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>
        Conventional routing follows predefined URL conventions.
    </li>

    <li>
        Routes are configured using app.MapControllerRoute().
    </li>

    <li>
        The default MVC route pattern is:
        controller/action/id
    </li>

    <li>
        {id?} represents an optional parameter.
    </li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

<p>
    Conventional Routing is a centralized routing approach in ASP.NET Core MVC
    where URL patterns are configured inside Program.cs
    to map requests to controllers and action methods.
</p>